      "authorId": "2009565615829467136",
      "media": [
        {
          "type": "video",
          "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2107860539112083456/img/rPANqN2MY8ld7PiB.jpg",
          "width": 718,
          "height": 1278,
          "previewUrl": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2107860539112083456/img/rPANqN2MY8ld7PiB.jpg:small",
          "videoUrl": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2107860539112083456/vid/avc1/718x1278/CcR-_2wzbaTlzFV9.mp4?tag=29",
          "durationMs": 45866
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2107809160402202671",
      "text": "I've been working on a passion project with Opus 5.5 and the UE 5.8 MCP: tearing down a house with an excavator. What a time to be alive :-)\n\nThe game itself isn't novel. What I want to share is how I adjusted my workflow during development to manage the complexity.\n\nAfter several rounds of random asks and tasks, it was hard to get a feel for the impact of all the decisions I'd made (or hadn't made because Opus made them for me). So I asked Opus 5.5 to turn all the .md files it had been creating into a website and treat it as the source of truth going forward.\n\nThe result is visual product documentation that serves as the authoritative spec. It's kept up to date and is fleshed out, and I iterate on it to iterate on the game.\n\nThis feels like a better flow for projects with lots of visuals, and a more intuitive way to get familiar with a project. Fast-forward a few steps into an ideal future and the engine, spec, and project start merging. You edit the project from within the visual spec via agents... and we've reinvented the editor ;-)\n\nThe difference is how that editor arranges context. LLMs would dynamically tailor the views, content, and tooling to the task at hand, including generating tools as needed. It becomes a mix of project documentation with live editing embedded etc.\n\nThe beauty of this approach is that exploring the project sparks joy in a way that reading plain .md files or browsing documents does not. Opus' ability to create compelling visuals and flows with taste is a game changer.",
